//! Bounded thread-safe LRU cache with TTL.
//!
//! Public API:
//! - `Cache<K, V>`: A generic LRU cache with TTL, thread-safe via Arc<Mutex<...>>.
//! - `Cache::new(capacity: usize, ttl: Duration, clock: Arc<dyn Clock>) -> Self`
//! - `Cache::get(&self, key: &K) -> Option<V>`
//! - `Cache::insert(&self, key: K, value: V)`
//! - `Cache::remove(&self, key: &K) -> Option<V>`
//! - `Cache::len(&self) -> usize`
//! - `Clock` trait: `now(&self) -> Instant`
//! - `SystemClock`: Uses std::time::Instant::now()
//! - `TestClock`: Deterministic clock for tests, with `advance(Duration)`
//!
//! Edge cases handled:
//! - Zero capacity: insert is a no-op, get returns None, len is 0.
//! - Negative capacity: not possible in Rust (usize), but zero is handled.
//! - TTL expiry: entries are lazily expired on access; expired entries are removed.
//! - LRU eviction: when capacity is reached, least recently used entry is evicted.
//! - Concurrent access: safe via Mutex; no unsafe code.
//! - Repeated remove: returns None if key not present.
//! - Insert over existing key: updates value and refreshes TTL and LRU position.
//!
//! Tradeoffs:
//! - Lazy expiry: expired entries are only removed when accessed or when capacity forces eviction.
//!   This avoids background threads but means `len()` may include expired entries until they are
//!   accessed or evicted. For strict TTL enforcement, a background sweeper would be needed.
//! - LRU tracking uses a Vec of keys in order of last access. This is O(n) for eviction but
//!   simple and correct. For large caches, a doubly-linked list would be more efficient.
//! - Clock is injected via Arc<dyn Clock> to allow deterministic testing.

use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use std::time::{Duration, Instant};

/// Trait for a clock that can be injected for deterministic testing.
